00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.630 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  on this first day of the month when rent  is due for so many americans we're going  to highlight the country's looming  eviction crisis  a federal moratorium on evictions runs  out at the end of this month  after that people who owe back rent  could be forced out of their homes at  the beginning of the year  an estimated 10 million renters were  behind owning a combined  57 billion with a b dollars in rent and  utilities  our national correspondent that's jerica  duncan shows us the toll on renters and  the building's owners  adjuster fernandez describes life these  days  as hanging on by a thread how have these  last 11 months been for you tough  i would say traumatic how much do you  owe in rent right now  close to twenty thousand close to twenty  thousand dollars  yeah for about four years fernandez has  been living with a roommate  in a second story apartment within a  house in queens new york  before the pandemic were you behind on  your rent no never  i've never had an eviction issue never  had a run  issue fernandez says it was a tough  decision at the beginning of the  pandemic  to not start a new job as a home health  aide but a necessary one  because of asthma and other medical  conditions  it's either i rest myself to die  and pay the rent and still die or  i stay home safe the risk is real for  fernandez whose mother was hospitalized  after contracting the virus  she literally was in critical condition  the doctors told me and the nurses told  me that she wasn't going to make it on  december 25th and we were preparing for  her  to just pass away thankfully she pulled  through  but it also was eye-opening for me like  i made the right decision for myself  currently fernandez is working a  part-time job from  home bringing in some twelve hundred  dollars a month  and fernandez's roommate lost her job at  the beginning of the pandemic  making their 1800 dollar monthly rent  payments  impossible what happens if you're  evicted  it's dead of winter right now i don't  know i really don't know  as state and federal moratoriums are  expected to expire in the next few  months  an estimated 30 to 40 million americans  are at risk of being evicted  black and latino people make up  approximately 80 percent of those facing  eviction  but it's not just renters landlords say  they're feeling the pressure too how  many of your tenants are behind right  now  they're all behind in one way or another  liz dunn owns six buildings in seattle  washington  it's made up of more than 20 small  businesses and nearly 30 apartments  my entire goal is to try and keep every  one of my tenants  in their space until we are all the way  through this  dunn admits that's been difficult she's  accepting less in rent from tenants  and has been directing them to grants  and loans how are you able to afford  your tenants at grace to say you know  what just chalk it up to  the pandemic and when you can start  paying me in full we'll move forward  well that's where the local banks come  in so they're affording me the same  grace  for example i'm getting help with  not having to pay principal on the loan  just making the interest payments  for renters who don't have a landlord  like dunn there may be some relief  coming soon  the federal government's emergency  rental assistance program went into  effect in january  25 billion dollars is being made  available to states  to help assist qualified households with  rent and utilities  but experts say it may not be enough  i've never been in a situation like this  for fernandez who recently applied for  food stamps  the future is uncertain they literally  said  you make three dollars more so i wasn't  poor enough to get assistance  but i didn't have enough to be able to  survive  what are you most concerned about when  you think about your situation and the  more than 40 million  americans in this country who are on the  verge of being kicked out of their  apartments  i'm thinking this is gonna further  create a  homeless crisis i want people to know  that this can happen to anybody  fernandez and her roommate were taken to  court housing court by the landlord we  did reach out to the landlord for  comment  but did not hear back tarika this has  got to feel overwhelming  what can be done to help those like ms  fernandez who are facing eviction  you know we spoke to a housing expert  and they said that this is a big issue  mainly because of that background and  that 25 billion dollars that the  government has set aside  is used to help those renters and  landlords with that background  but you have to apply and on top of that  it's really up to the states and local  governments as to how that money is  distributed experts say  the bottom line here the issue is best  addressed through policy changes and  making more affordable housing  available gail all right thank you very  much

